A||||6||Eric||Torque specs for control arms||||1||
Z||000000||Eric||04-09-2001||03:30 PM||||Does anyone know what the torque specs are for the 88 suspension?  I can't find my Haynes book, and I'm replacing all the bushings.  Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000001||DRH||04-09-2001||07:50 PM||||Front Specs from the GM manual:<BR>(ft/lbs)<P>UCA shaft to crossmember : 52 + 1/4 turn<BR>LCA to crossmember: 37 + 3/4 turn<BR>UCA ball joint to knuckle: 30-40<BR>LCA ball joint to knuckle: 26 + 1/2<BR>Tie rod at knuckle: 15 + 1/2 turn<BR>Hub/bearing to knuckle: 220<BR>Shock upper: 8<BR>Shock lower: 20<BR>Swaybar bushing clamp: 20<BR>Swaybar link: 12<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Eric||04-10-2001||12:00 AM||||Thanks, DRH.  Those are some highly technical specs + a quarter turn LOL  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  I don't know how the polys will affect the crush factor.  I think I'll just play it by ear.||||reg||1
Z||000003||DRH||04-10-2001||01:41 AM||||Yea, I've seen the 1/4 turn on things like head bolts... but suspension parts??? Seems like they would just say 60 or 65 lbs or whatever it works out to be.<P>I 'm working on redoing bushings, ball joints, brakes, etc. on my Formula right now. I just noticed that there is not a torque spec for the nuts on the end of the upper control arm shaft (the first spec abave is for the bolts that hold the shaft to the cross member). I re-checked the GM manual and the Haynes manual... Neither list it.<P>I believe these actually tighten against the steel sleeve, so bushing material shouldn't matter.<P>I'd guess 50 lbs or so from looking at them. With corrosion, they FELT like they were torqued to about 300 lbs taking them off... LOL...<P>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Eric||04-10-2001||12:13 PM||||You don't use locktite or anything, do you?||||reg||1
Z||000005||DRH||04-10-2001||01:34 PM||||Hmmm... I'm not sure. I'll check the manual tonight. If you do use it, make sure you use the stuff that will come apart again without heating to 100s of degrees...||||reg||1
Z||000006||Bob Englert||04-10-2001||01:55 PM||||The 88 Upper control arm bolts and paddle nuts were designed to be torqued and then stretched, thus the plus 1/4 turn.  The factory manual also stresses not to reuse the paddle nuts when servicing the front suspension.  The problem is you can't get new paddle nuts from GM or anywhere else as far as I know.<P>Just more challanges for the 88 owner to deal with....<P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
